  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1157 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nTonight\n\nWith the loss of diurnal mixing tonight, the wind gusts will \ngradually decline this evening and overnight. Even with that the\nsustained winds will remain in the 10 to 20 mph range. The \ncombination of warm air advection and the low-levels remaining \nwell-mixed, low temperatures will be warmer tonight. They will \nrange from the lower 30s to lower 40s. \n\nSunday15\n\nAs the day warms on Sunday morning, southerly wind gusts will \nincrease into the 25 to 35 mph range. In addition, this will mix\ndown warmer air from aloft to the surface. With the NBM running\non the cool side, used a mix of 1/3rd NBM and 2/3rds NBM 90th \npercentile to raise these temperatures up anywhere from 2 to 4F.\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will be in the lower and mid-60s.\n\nNext Week\n\nA 500 mb ridge will gradually build north into the region early\nnext week and then remain in control of the area through the\nweek. High temperatures will be mainly in the 60s through\nWednesday and then range from the upper 60s to mid-70s for the\nremainder of the week. Low temperatures will be in the 30s and\n40s through Monday night, 40s Tuesday night, and in the 40s and\n50s for the remainder of the week.",
